@startuml
title arc-remote-signer: components and trust boundaries
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam component {
  BackgroundColor #FDEEEE
  BorderColor #B54A4A
}
node "Validator host" {
  component "arc-node-consensus\n(remote-signer client)" as CL
}
node "Signer EC2 instance (parent, untrusted for key material)" {
  component "Proxy: SignerService\n0.0.0.0:10340\nTLS off by default, no client auth\ncaches encrypted key material" as PX
  node "Nitro Enclave (no network, no disk)" {
    component "EnclaveService (vsock 10350)\nplaintext Ed25519 key in memory\nNSM attestation doc (no nonce)" as EN
  }
}
cloud "AWS" {
  component "KMS key\npolicy: RecipientAttestation\nImageSha384 == PCR0" as KMS
  component "Secrets Manager\ngob{KMS blob, AES-GCM ct, nonce}" as SM
}
CL --> PX : Sign(bytes) / PublicKey\n(gRPC, 30 s timeout, 3 retries)
PX --> EN : SignMessage(alg, encrypted material, bytes)\ninsecure gRPC over vsock
PX --> KMS : GenerateDataKey / Decrypt\n+ Recipient(attestation doc)
KMS --> PX : CiphertextForRecipient\n(RSA-OAEP to enclave key)
PX --> SM : Get / PutSecretValue
note bottom of PX
Any process that can reach :10340,
or any process on the parent that can reach vsock,
obtains signatures. The enclave protects
key confidentiality, not key use.
end note
@enduml
